# Unity development rules

## General

- Use C# and Unity coding conventions.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Avoid `Update()` unless required; use events or coroutines when possible.

## API surface

- Use `[SerializeField]` instead of public fields for inspector-only data.
- Use `[RequireComponent]` when a script depends on another component.
- Cache component references in `Awake()`; do not use `GetComponent` in hot paths.

## Performance

- Use object pooling for frequently spawned objects (projectiles, particles, pooled managers).
- Avoid `FindObjectOfType` and similar in gameplay code; resolve references at init or via dependency injection / events.
- Avoid allocations in `Update()` and fixed timestep.

## Architecture

- Use ScriptableObjects for configuration and data (items, biomes, part definitions).
- Use events (EventBus) instead of polling for cross-system communication.
- Separate gameplay logic from UI logic.

## Naming

- Classes: PascalCase.
- Variables and parameters: camelCase.
- Private fields: _camelCase (or camelCase with [SerializeField]).

## Scene structure

Scenes should organize: Environment, Gameplay, UI, Managers (or per memory-bank/systemPatterns.md).

## Editor

- Prefer creating editor tools for repetitive or batch tasks; menu under RoobsWorld/.
